---
// 2026-08-18 visual port. docs/mockups .mbar — the bottom mobile action bar: navy "Request an
// assessment" beside an amber "Call now", fixed to the viewport bottom below 760px, always
// visible rather than scroll-revealed. That replaces the FINAL-v2 .sticky-bar and its
// IntersectionObserver, which the mockup has no equivalent for; the CSS-only version has no
// script at all, so it cannot fail to appear before hydration.
//
// The amber half is the second and last use of --ac-color-amber on any page, alongside the
// deadline band (owner ruling D1's "one rationed accent").
// HOMEPAGE VARIANT ADDED 2026-08-25 —
// docs/ASEPTACLEAN-CODEX-HOMEPAGE-BRIEF-V3-LEAN-SEVENSON.md §18: the audited bar showed three
// equal buttons, "That is not allowed for the homepage. The homepage must have a clear primary
// action. Preferred mobile behavior: primary: Tell Us About the Property; secondary: Call."
//
// ADAPTED, NOT DISABLED, AND NOT CHANGED GLOBALLY. §18 offers both routes and says do not alter
// shared behaviour for other pages without checking impact. This bar is imported once, by
// BaseLayout, so it ships on 45 routes — a global edit would silently restyle all of them, and
// switching it off on `/` would delete the most persistent conversion path the homepage has on
// mobile, which V3 §3 keeps. So the variant is scoped by route here rather than by a prop
// threaded through BaseLayout and every page that renders it.
//
// The text door is not deleted from the site. Doc 27 §8 requires the secondary CTA wherever the
// primary appears; on `/` it appears in the closing CTA and the footer, both of which are on the
// same page and both of which still render it. §18 explicitly permits it to "live inside
// request-assessment or a lower-priority text link" rather than as a third equal button.
// SERVICES-HUB VARIANT ADDED 2026-08-26, on the same reasoning and by the same mechanism as the
// homepage variant above. docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20A.10 sets the rule for this bar in
// one line — "only two actions" — and names the three-equal-button layout as the problem it
// resolves. `/services/` was rendering all three.
//
// SCOPED BY ROUTE, NOT CHANGED GLOBALLY, for the reason the homepage note already gives: this
// component is imported once by BaseLayout and ships on 45 routes, so editing the shared default
// would silently restyle every one of them. Two routes now opt into the two-action bar; the other
// 43 are untouched.
//
// The text door is not lost on `/services/`: doc 27 §8 requires the secondary CTA wherever the
// primary appears, and ServicesClose.astro renders it beside the closing primary on the same
// page — the same arrangement §18 permitted for `/`.
// HOARDING VARIANT ADDED 2026-08-26, third route to opt in, by the same mechanism and for the
// same reason. docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20A.10 governs this bar on a service page in one
// line — "only two actions" — and names the three-equal-button layout as the problem it
// resolves. `/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/` was rendering all three, on the page where a nervous
// buyer most needs one obvious next step rather than a choice of three.
//
// ESTATE CLEANOUT VARIANT ADDED for the /estate-cleanout-san-jose/ rebuild, fourth route to opt
// in, same mechanism and same §20A.10 rule. This page's closing CTA also renders Call and the
// SMS door beside the closing primary, so the text door is not lost here either.
//
// PROPERTY CLEANOUTS VARIANT ADDED for the /property-cleanouts-san-jose/ rebuild, fifth route to
// opt in, same mechanism and same §20A.10 rule ("only two actions"). This page's closing CTA also
// renders Call and the SMS door beside the closing primary, so the text door is not lost here
// either.
//
// DEEP CLEANING VARIANT ADDED for the /deep-cleaning-san-jose/ rebuild, sixth route to opt in,
// same mechanism and same §20A.10 rule. This page's closing CTA also renders Call and the SMS
// door beside the closing primary, so the text door is not lost here either.
//
// MOVE-OUT CLEANING VARIANT ADDED for the /move-out-cleaning-san-jose/ rebuild, seventh route to
// opt in, same mechanism and same §20A.10 rule ("only two actions"). This page's closing CTA also
// renders Call and the SMS door beside the closing primary, so the text door is not lost here
// either.
//
// RODENT DROPPINGS CLEANUP VARIANT ADDED for the /rodent-dropping-cleanup-san-jose/ rebuild,
// eighth route to opt in, same mechanism and same §20A.10 rule ("only two actions"). This page's
// closing CTA also renders Call and the SMS door beside the closing primary, so the text door is
// not lost here either.
//
// ANIMAL WASTE CLEANUP VARIANT ADDED for the /animal-waste-cleanup-san-jose/ rebuild, ninth route
// to opt in, same mechanism and same §20A.10 rule ("only two actions"). This page's closing CTA
// also renders Call and the SMS door beside the closing primary, so the text door is not lost
// here either.
//
// EXTREME CLEANING VARIANT ADDED pre-launch, tenth route to opt in. This page never joined the
// two-action bar during its own rebuild — a tracked inconsistency, since every other rebuilt
// service page already had — and pre-launch QA (§7 "two actions only, CALL | SERVICE-SPECIFIC
// ACTION") flagged the surviving three-action bar as the defect it always was. Same mechanism,
// same §20A.10 rule. This page's closing CTA also renders Call and the SMS door beside the
// closing primary, so the text door is not lost here either.
//
// SCOPED BY ROUTE, NOT CHANGED GLOBALLY, for the reason both notes above already give: this
// component is imported once by BaseLayout and ships on 45 routes, so editing the shared default
// would silently restyle every one of them. Ten routes now opt into the two-action bar; the
// other 35 are untouched. §20A.10's own example label set — "Call | Start Privately" — is not
// used, because the visible primary label is a single owner-approved string in site data
// (AGENTS.md §3) and re-labelling the button on one route would fork it.
//
// The text door is not lost on this route either: the hoarding page's closing CTA renders Call
// and the SMS door beside the closing primary, on the same page, exactly as `/` and `/services/`
// do.
import { site } from "@data/site";

const phoneHref = site.business.phoneUri;
const smsHref = site.business.smsUri;

const path = Astro.url.pathname.split(/[?#]/, 1)[0] || "/";
const isHome = path === "/" || path === "/index.html";
const isServicesHub = path === "/services/" || path === "/services/index.html";
const isHoarding =
  path === "/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/" || path === "/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/index.html";
const isEstateCleanout =
  path === "/estate-cleanout-san-jose/" || path === "/estate-cleanout-san-jose/index.html";
const isPropertyCleanouts =
  path === "/property-cleanouts-san-jose/" || path === "/property-cleanouts-san-jose/index.html";
const isDeepCleaning =
  path === "/deep-cleaning-san-jose/" || path === "/deep-cleaning-san-jose/index.html";
const isMoveOutCleaning =
  path === "/move-out-cleaning-san-jose/" || path === "/move-out-cleaning-san-jose/index.html";
const isRodentDroppings =
  path === "/rodent-dropping-cleanup-san-jose/" ||
  path === "/rodent-dropping-cleanup-san-jose/index.html";
const isAnimalWaste =
  path === "/animal-waste-cleanup-san-jose/" || path === "/animal-waste-cleanup-san-jose/index.html";
const isExtremeCleaning =
  path === "/extreme-cleaning-san-jose/" || path === "/extreme-cleaning-san-jose/index.html";
// All ten variants render one primary and one call. Kept as a single flag so the CSS has one
// selector to target and an eleventh route can join by adding one comparison above.
const isTwoAction =
  isHome ||
  isServicesHub ||
  isHoarding ||
  isEstateCleanout ||
  isPropertyCleanouts ||
  isDeepCleaning ||
  isMoveOutCleaning ||
  isRodentDroppings ||
  isAnimalWaste ||
  isExtremeCleaning;
---

<div class:list={["mbar", isTwoAction && "mbar--duo"]}>
  <a href="/request-assessment/">{site.offer.primaryCta}</a>
  {/* doc 27 §8 — the secondary CTA appears everywhere the primary does, and this bar is the
      most persistent primary on the site. Three slots at 390px is tight, so the bar drops to
      the short label below 400px (see the style block) rather than dropping the door.
      Suppressed on the two-action routes per §18 and §20A.10: see the notes above for where the
      door still appears on each of them. */}
  {!isTwoAction && smsHref && <a href={smsHref}>{site.offer.secondaryCta}</a>}
  {/* Suppression rule (AGENTS.md §3): never render an empty phone link. */}
  {phoneHref && <a href={phoneHref}>Call now</a>}
</div>

<style>
  .mbar {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .mbar {
      position: fixed;
      z-index: 80;
      right: 0;
      bottom: 0;
      left: 0;
      display: flex;
      border-top: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 15%);
    }

    .mbar a {
      display: flex;
      min-height: 48px;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      flex: 1;
      box-sizing: border-box;
      padding: 15px 8px;
      /* SAFE-AREA INSET, 2026-08-26. The bar is `bottom: 0` fixed, so on a device with a home
         indicator its lower band sat under the system gesture area — on the homepage that band
         is the bottom of the primary conversion action. The padding goes on the ANCHORS, not on
         .mbar: each half carries its own fill, and padding the container would have left a
         transparent strip of page showing through beneath both buttons. Each button therefore
         keeps its own colour all the way to the physical screen edge while its tappable text
         sits above the indicator. Zero on every device that reports no inset. */
      padding-bottom: calc(15px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      text-align: center;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    /* The primary carries the longest label of the three and gets the extra width, so
       "Request an assessment" stays on two lines at 390px instead of three. */
    .mbar a:first-child {
      flex: 1.5;
      background: var(--ac-color-navy-800);
      color: #fff;
    }

    /* doc 27 §8 secondary. Between the navy primary and the amber call, on warm white so it
       reads as the lighter-weight middle option rather than a third emphasis. Amber is still
       used exactly once here — owner ruling D1's rationed accent is unaffected. */
    .mbar a:nth-child(2) {
      background: var(--ac-color-warm-white);
      color: var(--ac-color-navy-900);
    }

    .mbar a:last-child {
      background: var(--ac-color-amber);
      color: #2a1d02;
    }

    /* TWO-ACTION ROUTES (`/`, `/services/` and `/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/`): one primary, one
       secondary — not two equal halves. The assessment CTA takes roughly two thirds of the bar
       and keeps the filled navy; Call becomes an outlined navy-on-white action, so the hierarchy
       is legible without a second saturated fill competing with it. Amber is not used here,
       which on `/` leaves the deadline band as its only appearance on the page and keeps owner
       ruling D1's rationed accent intact. */
    .mbar--duo a:first-child {
      flex: 2;
    }

    .mbar--duo a:last-child {
      border-top: 1px solid var(--ac-color-navy-800);
      background: var(--ac-color-paper);
      color: var(--ac-color-navy-900);
    }
  }

  /* Three slots below 400px. Tightening type and padding here keeps the bar one row tall on a
     390px viewport; above that the 760px values already fit. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .mbar a {
      padding: 12px 4px;
      padding-bottom: calc(12px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
      font-size: 0.78125rem;
      line-height: 1.25;
    }
  }
</style>
